---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="VMware Learning Platform: From Internal Tool to $1M+ SaaS • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["VMware", "VLP", "Lead Product Designer → Design Manager"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Tushar Roy // Case Study // VMware Learning Platform</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>From Internal Tool to $1M+ SaaS Product</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				VMware Learning Platform (VLP) was a powerful but aging internal
				tool built over two decades. It lacked a cohesive UX, modern
				architecture, and commercial market positioning.
			</p>
			<p>
				I led the product design transformation that reshaped it into a
				commercial SaaS offering—earning engineering trust, establishing
				a dedicated design team, and driving <strong>$1M+ in new ARR</strong>
				within the first year of commercial launch.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-vlp/enrollment.png"
					alt="VMware Learning Platform interface showing cloud-based lab environment and training management"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				VMware Learning Platform: browser-based hands-on cloud labs that
				scale from sales demos to enterprise certification training.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Designer → Design Manager</h4>
				<p>
					Market &amp; user research, product transformation, team
					leadership.
				</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"
					><time>2020</time> - <time>2023</time></small
				>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team &amp; scale</small>
				<h4>Cross-Functional SaaS Squad</h4>
				<p>
					1 Designer (me) → hired 1 designer, 14 Engineers, 1 PM, 2
					Solution Architects.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>$1M+ ARR // 30+ Clients</h4>
				<p>
					Scaled from internal utility to 30+ Fortune 500 customers and
					100K+ lab demos completed.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: CONTEXT & THE CHALLENGE                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">context</small>
				<h2>An engineering-first team with a 20-year legacy tool</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					In 2020, VMware had a battle-tested internal lab platform
					that had served the company well for two decades. Built
					entirely by engineers for engineers, the platform handled
					complex virtual machine orchestration with ease.
				</p>
				<p>
					However, leadership saw immense commercial potential in the
					cloud training space and mandated transforming it into a
					commercial SaaS product. The challenge was a brilliant
					engineering team naturally skeptical of design changes
					disrupting their proven workflows, paired with 20 years of
					accumulated technical debt.
				</p>

				<!-- Challenge Pillars Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CULTURE</small>
						<h4>Trust Deficit</h4>
						<p>
							As the first designer on the team, I needed to prove
							immediate value without slowing down established
							engineering momentum.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">PRODUCT</small>
						<h4>Commercial Pressure</h4>
						<p>
							Leadership expected rapid modernization to capture
							enterprise IT training demand against nimble SaaS
							competitors.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Legacy Before & Architecture Mapping -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-2.png"
								alt="Original VLP interface showing inconsistent legacy design patterns"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The original internal platform: technically
							powerful but dense, inconsistent, and intimidating
							for commercial buyers.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-13.png"
								alt="Information architecture and system mapping of legacy modules"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Information Architecture: untangling 20 years of
							nested features into a structured, modular hierarchy.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Bridging deep virtual machine complexity with consumer-grade
					SaaS usability.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: RESEARCH & ECOSYSTEM DISCOVERY             -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">research</small>
				<h2>Understanding the ecosystem: instructors, architects, and sales</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I initiated qualitative research across three critical
					stakeholder groups, supplemented by technical deep dives and
					competitive SaaS market positioning:
				</p>

				<!-- 3 Stakeholder Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ARCHITECTS</small>
						<h4>Environment Setup</h4>
						<p>
							Needed flexible multi-VM templates and rapid provisioning
							without command-line troubleshooting.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">INSTRUCTORS</small>
						<h4>Live Classroom Control</h4>
						<p>
							Required real-time student screen monitoring,
							breakout assistance, and progress tracking.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SALES TEAMS</small>
						<h4>Frictionless Demos</h4>
						<p>
							Needed 1-click customer access that worked in any
							HTML5 browser without VPNs or plugins.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: User Flow & Market Analysis -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-3.png"
								alt="User flow for creating a lab environment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Lab Creation Flow: mapping the multi-step journey of
							configuring virtual environments.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-6.png"
								alt="Market positioning analysis and commercial SaaS opportunities"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Commercial Opportunity: identifying enterprise
							buyer expectations for hands-on IT sandboxes.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Research confirmed that enterprise customers wanted
					accessible browser sandboxes without the administrative
					overhead of traditional VM setups.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- 3 Stakeholder Archetype Visuals -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-2-developers.svg"
								alt="Developers and student personas in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Learners &amp; Developers — Hands-on sandbox
							learners requiring instant console feedback.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-3-architects.svg"
								alt="Solution architects persona in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Lab Architects — Power users defining complex
							multi-tier network topologies.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-4-sales.svg"
								alt="Enterprise sales and field engineers persona in VLP"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Field Sales &amp; SEs — High-urgency users
							executing live customer demonstrations.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Architecting for diverse user archetypes across educational,
					technical, and commercial contexts.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: STRATEGY & EARNING TRUST                   -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">strategy</small>
				<h2>Building trust through incremental sprint wins</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					In an engineering-led culture, suggesting a massive "ground-up
					redesign" is the fastest way to hit a wall. Instead of
					pushing disruptive redesigns, I proposed <strong
						>small, high-impact design refinements</strong
					> sprint-by-sprint.
				</p>
				<p>
					By addressing legibility, alignment, and usability in
					features engineers were actively building, I proved that
					design accelerated their velocity rather than impeding it.
				</p>

				<!-- Strategy Callout Box -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow mb-2">EARNING TECHNICAL TRUST</small>
					<h4>Incremental Polish over Big-Bang Overhauls</h4>
					<p>
						Engineers embraced design when they saw that component
						reusability made front-end development faster. Trust was
						built commit by commit, proving that good UX reduced QA
						re-work.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Sprint Tweaks & Team Alignment -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-4.png"
								alt="Iterative UI improvements and sprint-by-sprint design polish"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Sprint-by-Sprint Polish: micro-improvements to table
							densities, status badges, and action toolbars.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-5-trust.avif"
								alt="Engineering collaboration and design integration milestone"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Engineering Collaboration: establishing shared design
							tokens and UI component libraries with dev leads.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Demonstrating immediate, friction-free value earned the team's
					unanimous backing for larger strategic transformations.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: THE TURNING POINT - CLASSROOM POC          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">innovation</small>
				<h2>The Classroom Experience: design-led breakthrough</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The pivotal moment occurred when I was invited to design a
					brand-new <strong>Classroom Experience</strong> module from
					scratch. Rather than designing it as an isolated feature, I
					used it as a showcase for what the modern SaaS version of
					VLP could be.
				</p>
				<p>
					I mapped the complete instructor-student journey, combining
					real-time VM telemetry with intuitive moderation controls:
				</p>

				<!-- Full Width Journey Map -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-11.png"
							alt="End-to-end user journey of VLP instructors and students"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						End-to-end User Journey: mapping session initialization,
						student lab onboarding, live interventions, and post-class
						evaluations.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Side by Side: Instructor View & Student View -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-12.png"
								alt="Instructor classroom management interface"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Instructor Cockpit: live bird's-eye view of all
							active student VMs with instant intervention triggers.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-10.png"
								alt="Student interactive lab sandbox environment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Student Sandbox: dual-pane interface pairing lab
							manual instructions with live HTML5 terminal access.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					The Classroom POC proved that enterprise virtual labs could
					feel as smooth and responsive as modern consumer web apps.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: SOLUTION - VLP LITE & COMMERCIAL LAUNCH    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">solution</small>
				<h2>Designing VLP Lite for commercial enterprise customers</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					To address the commercial market opportunity, I designed <strong
						>VLP Lite</strong
					>—a streamlined, self-service offering tailored for rapid
					enterprise onboarding while preserving full hypervisor power.
				</p>

				<!-- 4 Principles Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">DISCLOSURE</small>
						<h4>Progressive Complexity</h4>
						<p>
							Advanced networking settings tucked behind clean
							defaults, allowing non-technical admins to launch
							labs in minutes.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LANGUAGE</small>
						<h4>Simplified Terminology</h4>
						<p>
							Replaced internal engineering jargon with
							standardized enterprise training terms.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ACCESSIBILITY</small>
						<h4>Universal HTML5 Access</h4>
						<p>
							Any browser, any device—zero plugins, VPNs, or client
							software installations required.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CONFIDENCE</small>
						<h4>Commercial Polish</h4>
						<p>
							A premium, modern visual language that built
							immediate trust with Fortune 500 decision makers.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- 3 Highlights Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-12-browser.webp"
								alt="HTML5 in-browser lab access"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Universal Web Access — High-performance HTML5
							rendering accessible across laptops and tablets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-13-modular.webp"
								alt="Modular UI architecture"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Modular Architecture — Resilient components
							adapting to self-paced learning and live seminars.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/vlp-14-console.webp"
								alt="Advanced virtual machine console controls"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Enterprise Control — Low-latency virtual console
							control with multi-monitor support.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Three foundational pillars of VLP Lite: universal access,
					modular components, and deep virtual machine control.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- VLP Lite Interface Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-6">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-19.png"
							alt="VLP Lite enterprise-ready interface"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						VLP Lite: enterprise-ready design unveiled and tested
						successfully at VMworld with Fortune 500 customers.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: SCALING THE PLATFORM & TEAM                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">system</small>
				<h2>Transforming core modules and scaling the design team</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Following the commercial traction of VLP Lite, I
					transitioned into a <strong>Design Management role</strong>,
					hired a dedicated product designer, and led the end-to-end
					redesign of every major platform module:
				</p>

				<!-- 6-Grid of Platform Modules -->
				<div
					class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4"
				>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-20.png"
								alt="LabBuilder multi-VM topology designer"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. LabBuilder — Visual canvas for configuring
							complex multi-VM network topologies.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-16.png"
								alt="Global event management and scheduling dashboard"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Event Management — Scheduling multi-regional
							training sessions and capacity allocation.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/Dashboard_1.png"
								alt="Executive and operational lab metrics dashboard"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Dashboard — Real-time analytics tracking active
							labs, seat utilization, and host health.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/Mockup-2.png"
								alt="Multi-device lab mockup on modern hardware"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							04. Cross-Device — Responsive design enabling lab
							oversight from tablets and desktops.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-1.png"
								alt="Enterprise lab catalog and enrollment portal"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							05. Lab Catalog — Frictionless course discovery and
							one-click sandbox enrollment.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-17.png"
								alt="Student progress tracking and analytics"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							06. Student Analytics — Real-time milestone
							completion rates and comprehension metrics.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					A complete enterprise SaaS suite: spanning topology
					authoring, global event operations, and granular learner
					analytics.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: RESULTS & OUTCOME                          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article id="outcome" class="case-section border-b-0 pb-0">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h2>Results</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The transformation of VMware Learning Platform proved that
					thoughtful product design can unlock massive commercial
					value from legacy engineering assets.
				</p>
				<p>
					What began as an internal utility with skeptical stakeholders
					became an official VMware commercial product driving
					significant recurring revenue and delighting Fortune 500
					enterprises worldwide.
				</p>

				<!-- Impact Scorecard Grid: 4 Metric Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">REVENUE</small>
						<h4>$1M+ ARR</h4>
						<p>Generated in year 1 of commercial launch.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CUSTOMERS</small>
						<h4>30+ Clients</h4>
						<p>Fortune 500 enterprises onboarded.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SESSIONS</small>
						<h4>12K+ Delivered</h4>
						<p>Live global enterprise training sessions.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SCALE</small>
						<h4>100K+ Labs</h4>
						<p>Hands-on sandbox demo environments run.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Usability and Market Adoption Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 mb-8">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-vlp/trfolio-vlp-18.png"
							alt="Usability test results and commercial adoption curve"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						Commercial adoption and usability validation: confirming
						95%+ task completion rates among non-technical enterprise
						administrators.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Key Learnings Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">LEARNING 01</small>
						<h4>Trust First</h4>
						<p>
							Respecting existing engineering workflows and
							technical complexity is prerequisite to influencing
							product architecture.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
